# Nothing here is required.
#
# On Vercel the AI Gateway authenticates itself through OIDC, so a deployed app
# answers with no key configured at all. Locally, either run `vercel env pull`
# to get a short-lived OIDC token, or set a Gateway key:
# AI_GATEWAY_API_KEY=

# Or point the agent at any OpenAI-compatible endpoint instead: OpenRouter,
# Groq, Together, or Ollama and vLLM on your own hardware.
# OPENAI_COMPATIBLE_BASE_URL=http://localhost:11434/v1
# OPENAI_COMPATIBLE_API_KEY=ollama
# OPENAI_COMPATIBLE_MODEL=llama3.2

# Raises Firecrawl's crawl limits. Ingest works without it on the free keyless
# tier, which gives 1,000 page credits a month.
# FIRECRAWL_API_KEY=

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Channels. Every one of these is optional and none is read unless you wire the
# matching adapter up. The names come from the adapters' own options, so this
# is the shopping list: create the app, put the values in .env.local, point the
# platform's webhook at your tunnel.
#
# One thing that catches people on every platform: a webhook signature is
# computed over the RAW body. A framework that parses JSON before you verify
# has already changed the bytes, and the signature will never match.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# WhatsApp Business, and Messenger and Instagram, which share Meta's scheme.
# The verify token is one you invent and type into Meta's dashboard; the app
# secret is Meta's. WHATSAPP_PHONE_ID is the number's id, not the number.
# META_APP_SECRET=
# META_VERIFY_TOKEN=
# WHATSAPP_PHONE_ID=
# WHATSAPP_TOKEN=
# Only for template sends, and it is the business account rather than the
# number. A template can only be sent from a number on its own account.
# WHATSAPP_WABA_ID=
#
# One trap worth checking before blaming your code, hit twice on two Meta
# products: the dashboard's per-account "Webhook Subscription: On" toggle and
# the app-level field subscription are different things, and only the second
# decides what Meta sends. A verified callback, a green toggle and a successful
# POST to /{page-id}/subscribed_apps can all be true while nothing arrives.
# Check it, and assert the fields are not empty:
#
#   GET /{app-id}/subscriptions?access_token={app-id}|{app-secret}
#   -> object: page   fields: (none)      <- delivers nothing
#
#   POST /{app-id}/subscriptions
#     object=page&fields=messages&callback_url=...&verify_token=...
#
# Getting these is about twenty minutes and costs nothing. Create an app at
# developers.facebook.com, add the WhatsApp product, and a test number is
# generated for you. No business verification: Meta only requires that for
# Advanced Access, which a test number does not use. The dashboard token is
# short-lived; a System User token from business.facebook.com is the long-lived
# one and needs no verification either.
#
# One step the dashboard never mentions: the number it hands you is not
# registered with the Cloud API, and every send fails with "(#133010) Account
# not registered" until it is. Once, with any six digits as the pin:
#
#   curl -X POST "https://graph.facebook.com/v25.0/$WHATSAPP_PHONE_ID/register" \
#     -H "Authorization: Bearer $WHATSAPP_TOKEN" \
#     -H "Content-Type: application/json" \
#     -d '{"messaging_product":"whatsapp","pin":"271828"}'
#
# A test number also messages only the recipients you add to it, five at most,
# each verified by a code sent to that handset.
#
# Note for later, not for setup: from 1 October 2026 Meta charges for service
# messages, which is what a reply to a customer is. They have been free since
# November 2024.

# Slack. The signing secret verifies the request; the bot token sends. Slack
# rejects a request whose timestamp is more than five minutes old, which is
# also the replay test on the card.
# SLACK_SIGNING_SECRET=
# SLACK_BOT_TOKEN=

# Telegram. One token does both directions.
# TELEGRAM_BOT_TOKEN=
# The cheapest credential here: message @BotFather, send /newbot, done in a
# minute with no account and no verification. Telegram signs nothing, so
# TELEGRAM_SECRET is one you invent, pass to setWebhook, and it sends back on
# every update. It is the only thing between your bot and anyone who guesses
# the URL, so treat it like a password rather than like a name.
# TELEGRAM_SECRET=

# Discord. The public key verifies an Ed25519 signature, and Discord will not
# save an endpoint that fails its PING, so this one tells you immediately.
# DISCORD_PUBLIC_KEY=
# DISCORD_BOT_TOKEN=
# Instagram, which shares this app secret and this signature scheme, and is the
# expensive one. None of what follows is visible from the dashboard, which
# shows the account connected and the webhook switched on either way.
#
# Meta's Instagram webhooks documentation states twice that the app must be set
# to Live for notifications to be sent at all, and the requirement table for
# Business Login for Instagram also asks for Advanced Access and business
# verification. Messenger, on the same app and the same endpoint, delivers to
# an unpublished development app quite happily, because there the sender is the
# Facebook account that administers the app and so already holds a role.
#
# Tested to exhaustion on 2026-08-30 rather than assumed. Standard Access is
# automatic and needs no verification, but only for app users holding a role,
# and the only way to give an Instagram account one is the Instagram Tester
# role, which belongs to the Basic Display API that Meta retired in December
# 2024 and whose form no longer saves. Budget for a verified company before
# promising anybody Instagram support.
#
# One trap that costs an afternoon on its own: a message from an account that
# does not follow the business lands in Instagram's message requests folder,
# not the inbox, and produces no webhook. Nothing in the dashboard says so.
# INSTAGRAM_ACCOUNT_ID=
# INSTAGRAM_TOKEN=

# Messenger. The page token, not the app token, and it is the page's id rather
# than its name or vanity URL. Instagram replies through the page it is linked
# to, so this token serves both unless a separate one has been issued.
# MESSENGER_PAGE_ID=
# MESSENGER_PAGE_TOKEN=

# DISCORD_APP_ID=
#
# Free, and the only one here where a leaked key is not a breach: you hold the
# public half, so nothing you have can forge a request. Two things catch people
# out. The portal will not save the endpoint until it has sent an unsigned PING
# and watched it be rejected, so the server has to be running first. And a
# slash command must be registered before anyone can type it, which is a
# separate API call; a global one can take an hour to appear in clients, while
# a per-guild one is instant.
#
# Verified 2026-08-29. Saving the URL sends two requests, a correctly signed
# PING that must be answered and a deliberately invalid one that must be
# rejected, and it refuses to save unless both behave. So the server has to be
# up first. That is the opposite of Meta, which saves whatever you type and
# fails later.

# Twilio, for SMS and voice. The signature covers the exact public URL Twilio
# called, so a tunnel with a changing subdomain fails every time. Use a stable
# one and pass it as `publicUrl`.
# TWILIO_ACCOUNT_SID=
# TWILIO_AUTH_TOKEN=
# TWILIO_FROM=+15550000000
# TWILIO_PUBLIC_URL=https://your-stable-tunnel.example

# Microsoft Teams, through the Bot Framework. The app password is exchanged
# for a token before each send, so a wrong one fails on reply rather than on
# receipt.
#
# Two things that are not in any quickstart. An app registration is not enough
# on its own: without a service principal in the tenant the token request fails
# with AADSTS7000229, which reads like a bad secret and is not. And the bot
# resource stores your endpoint by address rather than verifying a signature,
# so a new tunnel means re-pointing it, and a stale one is silence rather than
# an error.
#
# TEAMS_TENANT_ID is only for a single-tenant bot, which is what Microsoft now
# recommends; multi-tenant creation was retired in July 2025.
# TEAMS_APP_ID=
# TEAMS_APP_PASSWORD=
# TEAMS_TENANT_ID=

# Sunshine Conversations, which is Zendesk's messaging platform and an
# aggregator: one integration reaches WhatsApp, Messenger, Instagram, Telegram,
# LINE, WeChat, Viber and SMS. The webhook secret is generated when you create
# the webhook and returned once, in that response; the key pair is separate and
# is what authenticates the reply going back out.
#
# Nothing is signed here. The secret arrives in X-API-Key and comparing it is
# the whole check, so it is a password rather than a public identifier.
# SUNSHINE_APP_ID=
# SUNSHINE_WEBHOOK_SECRET=
# SUNSHINE_KEY_ID=
# SUNSHINE_KEY_SECRET=

# Inbound email. The shared secret is a header your provider is configured to
# send, since there is no standard signature scheme for inbound mail.
# EMAIL_FROM=support@your-domain.example
# EMAIL_WEBHOOK_HEADER=x-webhook-secret
# EMAIL_WEBHOOK_SECRET=

# Voice, when you want speech rather than text. ElevenLabs Agents own the call
# and call this library as a webhook tool mid-conversation, so what matters is
# the bearer token their tool presents, which you invent and configure on both
# sides. Their conversation simulator mocks webhook tools and hands the agent
# the literal string "Tool Called.", so a simulated test reports a working
# integration as broken. Drive a real conversation instead.
# ELEVENLABS_TOOL_TOKEN=
# ELEVENLABS_API_KEY=
#
# The same agent can take a call from the widget rather than from a phone. That
# needs the agent's id as well as the key, because the browser is handed a
# signed URL for one specific agent and nothing else.
#
# Setting it up, once:
#   1. Create an agent in the ElevenLabs dashboard.
#   2. Add a webhook tool on it pointing at POST /api/voice/knowledge, with a
#      `question` string parameter, and a secret header carrying the token
#      above. That is how it answers from your content instead of guessing.
#   3. Paste its system prompt from `elevenLabsSystemPrompt()`, which tells it
#      to answer only from what the tool returns.
#   4. Put the agent id here and add data-call="/api/voice/token" to the widget.
#
# Worth knowing before the bill arrives: a call is billed per minute while it is
# open, and the button is public. The token route is rate limited for that
# reason, and the limit is per instance rather than global.
# ELEVENLABS_AGENT_ID=
#
# A call is the one place a slow model is not merely annoying. The tool above
# has a timeout in seconds and the agent goes quiet when it passes, which the
# caller hears as the line dropping. Measured here: a local reasoning model took
# 53 seconds for one question, and a smaller local one 37, against a 30 second
# timeout. Published benchmarks put retrieval at 50 to 300ms and generation at
# 500 to 8000ms, so the model is the whole problem and a fast host is the fix.
#
# These three point voice at its own provider, separate from the one answering
# chat, because the fast option is usually somewhere else entirely.
# The `openai` in both the URL and the model name is not an OpenAI account.
# The path is Groq's OpenAI-compatible endpoint, and `gpt-oss` is OpenAI's
# open-weights release, which Groq hosts. One key, theirs.
#
# Pick on tokens per second, not on how clever the model is: on a call the
# answer is two sentences and the only thing that matters is how soon it
# starts. Groq publishes 1000 t/s for the 20b and 500 for the 120b, and the
# 20b is also the cheaper of the two. Measured here at 0.49s and 0.61s for the
# same question.
#
# Check the model list against your own key before trusting a name. Some
# models are Enterprise-only and simply absent on a free key, so a name copied
# from the docs can 404 without being deprecated.
# VOICE_BASE_URL=https://api.groq.com/openai/v1
# VOICE_MODEL=openai/gpt-oss-20b
# VOICE_API_KEY=

# Object storage for attachments, and the secret that signs the keys the
# upload route hands out. Any long random string.
# S3_BUCKET=
# S3_ENDPOINT=
# S3_ACCESS_KEY_ID=
# S3_SECRET_ACCESS_KEY=
# S3_REGION=auto
# RECOURSE_UPLOAD_SECRET=
